course: dictionary
lesson: 11238
title: Words for the people you work with
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: A workmate is a person you work with in the same job or place. Read the sentence: «Some retired people miss the routine, their workmates and the feeling of being useful.» Workmates are people from the job, so they are part of the daily routine that people miss when they stop working.
explain: The clues help you. The sentence lists three things retired people miss: the routine, their workmates and the feeling of being useful. Workmates sits in the middle of that list, so it must mean a group of people, not a place or a thing.
explain: The word is made of two parts: work and mate. A mate is a friend, so a workmate is a friend from work. Some other words mean the same thing: in the USA people more often say «coworker» or «colleague».
fact: A workmate is a person you work with in the same job or place.
fact: Workmates is the plural: one workmate, many workmates.
fact: Workmate is mainly used in the UK, Australia, New Zealand and Ireland.
fact: In the USA people more often say «coworker» or «colleague».
fact: Workmates can help each other, or sometimes annoy each other, in everyday life and in stories.
exercise: «Some retired people miss the routine, their workmates and the feeling of being useful.» What is a workmate? | A kind of tool used in the garden | A friend you only see at weekends | A place where you go to rest | *A person you work with in the same job or place
exercise: Ella's dad in Sydney says, «I'm having lunch with my workmates today.» Who is he having lunch with? | The people who sell food at the shop | His old school friends | *People he works with | His cousins from the country
exercise: An American says, «My coworker helped me fix the printer.» What would a British person say instead? | My neighbour's dog | *My workmate | My grandad | My teacher
test: In «Some retired people miss ... their workmates», what kind of people are workmates? | Animals on a farm | *People you work with | People who live next door | Strangers on a bus
test: Which word means nearly the same as workmate? | Neighbour | Passenger | *Colleague | Stranger
exam: Which sentence uses workmate correctly? | I ate my workmate for breakfast. | The workmate flew across the sky. | *Dad laughed with his workmates over lunch in the staff room. | The workmate of the sun rose over the hill.
